    "description": "Iranian video game competitions include domestic events combining football simulations and mobile games, alongside participation in international esports tournaments. Event reports and match records reveal different competitive formats, but do not establish a comprehensive national participation total.",
    "tags": ["Video games", "Competitions", "Esports", "Mobile games", "Counter-Strike"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:01:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:01:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Video Game Competitions in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iranian gaming competitions through documented domestic tournaments, provincial participation, and international women's Counter-Strike matches."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-competitive-settings",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different games, different competitive settings",
        "content": "Competitive gaming in Iran is best understood through specific events rather than a single national league table. A domestic competition may combine a football simulation played at a venue with contests inside Iranian mobile games. An international esports championship instead records national representatives competing within a particular game's tournament structure. The domestic Champions Cup and Iran's participation in the women's Counter-Strike championship illustrate these distinct settings. Their entrants and results should not be combined into one measure of competitive gaming. [s1][s2]\n\nThis distinction also separates the player from the developer. Winning a match demonstrates performance under that competition's rules; it does not establish that the game itself was developed in Iran. The domestic event discussed here included both an international football title and Iranian mobile games. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "domestic-champions-cup",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A domestic cup with provincial connections",
        "content": "Honaronline's closing report on the eighth Iranian Video Games Champions Cup describes provincial selection feeding into a national event in Tehran. Published on 12 Aban 1402 in the Iranian Solar Hijri calendar, it reports recognition for winners across 20 Iranian mobile games and the FIFA 23 competition. The report identifies mobile operator Hamrah-e Aval as a supporter. These details show how a gaming event could connect mobile-game participation, a venue-based football tournament, and commercial sponsorship. [s1]\n\nThe football final brought together Parham Hamrang from Gilan Province and Nima Sadeghi from Tehran Province; Honaronline reported that Hamrang won on penalties. This is a concrete example of provincial representation reaching a Tehran final, rather than evidence that all provinces had equal access or equivalent competitive infrastructure. [s1]\n\nParticipation claims require particular care. The report relays a minister's statement about more than a million participants, but provides no audited counting method distinguishing unique people, accounts, repeated entries, or participation across different games. That claim is therefore not used here as a numerical observation or compared with attendance at a physical final. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "international-match-records",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "International competition leaves a different evidence trail",
        "content": "A more granular record exists for Iran's women's Counter-Strike team at the 2024 IESF Women World Championship. IESF is the International Esports Federation. HLTV's match archive records Iran facing Canada on 12 November 2024 in a Group C elimination match played on a local-area network, commonly abbreviated LAN. Canada won the series by two maps to one. The archive supplies the map-selection sequence, individual map results, and player statistics. [s2]\n\nThe federation's own Gamer Pass directory also identifies Aida Sharif Nia, competing as iMoon, with Iran's women's Counter-Strike participation in 2024. Together, these records document women's involvement through named competitors and actual matches. They do not establish the size of the wider female player population or the accessibility of a professional career. [s3]\n\nThe useful conclusion is specific: Iran has documented domestic competitions and international competitive participation, including women's events. Assessing the scene's development requires consistent records of entrants, qualification rules, completed matches, and paid prizes, rather than treating promotional scale or a single result as a national trend. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
