    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran's online entertainment services include video-sharing platforms and subscription catalogues of films and series. Their differences in content supply and viewing experience coexist with licensing requirements, editorial restrictions, and unequal access to domestic and international services.",
    "tags": ["Streaming", "Video sharing", "Aparat", "Filimo", "Namava", "Filmnet"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:01:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:01:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Online Entertainment Platforms in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Aparat, subscription streaming, Iranian platform ownership, and the licensing and access conditions shaping online entertainment."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "platform-types",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Video sharing and subscription viewing",
        "content": "Online entertainment in Iran includes services with different relationships to their audiences. Aparat is a video-sharing platform, while Filimo, Namava, and Filmnet are associated with subscription video on demand, often abbreviated SVOD: viewers subscribe to access a selected catalogue rather than follow a television broadcast schedule. Freedom House discusses Aparat's role in Iran's online environment, while Azade Falaki's research examines catalogue curation and regulation across the subscription services. [s1][s2]\n\nThese categories matter when interpreting claims about popularity. A visit to a video-sharing website, a registered account, and a paying subscription measure different things. None automatically identifies a unique viewer, and an account may be used within a household. This page therefore offers no combined platform audience total or ranking based on incompatible measures.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "business-connections",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connections across the entertainment business",
        "content": "The Asian Contents & Film Market's 2025 Iran report identifies Filimo as belonging to Saba Idea, which also owns Aparat and Cinema Ticket. The latter is a ticket-selling service, making the connection broader than online viewing alone. The same report describes Filmnet as acquired by the Golrang investment group. These are reported ownership relationships, rather than evidence that the services have identical catalogues, users, or editorial policies. [s3]\n\nThe report also describes streaming as a distribution outlet for work that does not obtain a theatrical release. This connects platforms to the production economy: online distribution can provide an additional route to viewers, although the existence of that route does not demonstrate that a particular production recovered its costs. Platform catalogues should consequently be read as commercial and editorial selections, rather than a complete inventory of Iranian screen production. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "catalogues-and-regulation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A selectable catalogue is still an edited catalogue",
        "content": "Falaki's 2025 study examines how subscription services curate and alter content within Iran's regulatory environment. It identifies SATRA, an audiovisual regulator affiliated with Islamic Republic of Iran Broadcasting, as part of the licensing structure. The study distinguishes production permissions from authorization to distribute completed works. Its analysis of foreign material includes censorship in Filimo's presentation of The Queen's Gambit. [s2]\n\nThe implication is that seeing a familiar foreign title in a catalogue does not establish that it is the same edition available elsewhere. Selection, editing, and authorization shape what subscribers can actually watch. The study explains a documented regulatory setting; it should not be treated as a complete statement of every platform's rules or every subsequent licensing decision. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "access-and-interpretation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Access conditions help shape the market",
        "content": "Freedom House's 2024 assessment describes a structural advantage for domestic services: Aparat benefited from less expensive internet tariffs, while international services faced blocking. It also reports restrictions on politically or socially sensitive content on Aparat. This helps explain why a service's role cannot be understood solely through its features or catalogue. Network policy and permissible speech also affect the choices available to audiences. [s1]\n\nThose findings belong to the report's assessment period. They do not establish uninterrupted access, current prices, or a particular service's availability on every Iranian network at the editorial cutoff. The most useful way to understand the sector is to keep the layers distinct: who operates a platform, how content reaches its catalogue, what viewers pay for, and whether they can reliably reach it. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "title": "Iran: Freedom on the Net 2024 Country Report",
